Ladybird never caches a missing property on a shape for a number key,
since a shape doesn’t record indexed properties. Gecko lets only names
and symbols reach its named-property stubs, missing-slot ones included
(GetPropIRGenerator::tryAttachStub, after ValueToNameOrSymbolId), so
an index goes to the element stubs. V8’s LookupIterator treats an
array-index name as an element lookup, and KeyedLoadIC::Load turns a
string key that’s an array index into one (String::AsArrayIndex), so
LoadHandler::LoadNonExistent only ever caches a missing named property.
And WebKit’s getOwnPropertySlot() sends an index name on to its
getOwnPropertySlotByIndex() (parseIndex, in JSObjectInlines.h), so no
Structure ever answers for an index.

Node gives the same results as Ladybird for every case in the test.
